was worried about f5 here but assumed it probably wasn't great for black long term and I was right.}  15. Qf4! {after f5, qf4 controls the dark squares very well and any kingside attack by black would be superficial. qf4 comes with the idea of f3 too to kick the knight away and also cover the g3 square. } Ndf6 16. f3!) 15. g4?! {[%clk 0:08:53.1] I played this as I knew this was a common theme in certain lines out of this opening, but that was really the only reason I chose this, it's actually an innacuracy in this position - don't rely on opening plans unless the same position comes up on the board, play chess before trying to memorise positions and force those ideas on the board. This is also not so favouorable because of the number of minor pieces on the board - 2 minor pieces have already been traded, which makes any spatial advantage less effective. This idea works better when more minor pieces are on the board and haven't been traded.}  (15. Qe2! {the engine wants to put the bishop all the way back to e1! to prepare f3 and a queenside pawn expansion!}  Qc7 16. Be1 {[%cal Ga2a3,Gb2b4]} dxc4  (16... h5  17. Rc1) 17. Bxc4) h6 {[%clk 0:12:34.9] } 16. Qf4? {[%clk 0:08:20.9] }  (16. Qg2! {more accurate was Qg2 so that h3 is covered after f3 ng5. }  Qb6 17. Ba3 Qd8 18. f3 Ng5 19. h4 {with the queen on g2 there is no nh3 +. This is what I missed when I played qf4. }) Qb6 {[%clk 0:12:28.6] } 17. Ba3 {[%clk 0:08:13.1] } Qd8 {[%clk 0:10:50.7] } 18. f3 {[%clk 0:07:32.4] } Nd6?? {[%clk 0:10:30] a one move blundner ends the game}  (18... Ng5  19. Qg3 {[%cal Gh3h4,Gf3f4]the line I had in mind} dxc4 20. Bc2 Nd5 21. f4 Nh7 22. e4 Nc7 {[%cal Ga3b4,Gb4c3]}) 19. Bxd6 {[%clk 0:07:32.7] } 1-0

